      "color": "#325",
      "bgcolor": "#446",
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Turbo LoRA: 4 steps instead of 20\n\nA distill by larryvrh on top of the same base. Sampling gets almost four times\nfaster and the full run three times: 11:08 became 3:45 on the same clip and\nseed.\n\nWeights: `minimax_h3_turbo_4step_ema_ckpt850.safetensors`\nNodes: `Larryvrh/ComfyUI-MiniMax-H3-Turbo`\n\n## The two nodes that change everything\n\n`MiniMax H3 Turbo LoRA` applies the weights and repairs time conditioning on\nits own. Pruned bases collapse the time embedder into a small curve, and the\nnode detects that and re-injects 51 adaln modules at run time. One LoRA covers\nfull, int8 and pruned bases alike.\n\n`MiniMax H3 Turbo Sampler` runs video and audio on two different flow\nschedules (shift 12 and 3 are baked in). A stock sampler over-steps the audio\nat 4 steps and breaks it, so this replacement is not optional.\n\n## Knobs\n\n**LoRA strength** is the main dial, default 1.0. Blurry with motion smear, push\nit up to 1.05 or 1.2. Over-sharp grain and artifacts, pull it down to 0.8 or\n0.95.\n\n**Leave low_vram ON.** The name misleads: it is not a memory setting, it picks\nhow the LoRA reaches the model. Off, the LoRA runs as a hook on all 208 modules\nand its delta applies at full precision every pass. On, the delta folds into the\nweights once, and since this base is int8 part of it rounds away, so merged is a\nweaker LoRA in practice.\n\nThat is what you want here. One frame, one seed, close-up face at 1344x768:\n\n| config | steps | skin | detail energy | run |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| bypass, strength 1.0 | 4 | scaly | 3.38 | 3:56 |\n| bypass, strength 1.0 | 8 | still scaly | 3.38 | 6:41 |\n| bypass, strength 1.0 | 12 | worst of all | | 9:36 |\n| bypass, strength 0.85 | 4 | clean | | 3:51 |\n| **merged, strength 1.0** | **4** | **clean** | **2.57** | **3:46** |\n| no LoRA (reference) | 20 | clean | 2.46 | 14:36 |\n\nDetail energy is laplacian energy over the clip. Bypass lands 37% above the\nno-LoRA reference, merged lands within 4% of it, so the extra sharpness in\nbypass is the artefact rather than detail. On a close-up it turns skin into a\nregular lattice.\n\n**More steps make it worse.** 8 and 12 kept the lattice and hardened it. Step\ncount is not the dial.\n\nWhat merged costs: large-scale motion runs about 9% below the 20-step reference,\nso the action is slightly calmer. Nothing else measurable.\n\n**Steps.** Four is enough on ckpt850. More is allowed and helps a little. Keep\nthe scheduler on `simple`.\n\n**The author's own warning.** This is a preview, not a finished model. Training\nis paused because of plastic-looking skin and over-sharpened grain. Watch faces\nin close-ups.\n\n## Going back to 20 steps\n\nFour actions, all inside this graph:\n\n1. Ctrl+B on the `Turbo LoRA` node (it goes to bypass);\n2. Ctrl+B on `Sigma Shift` (it turns back on);\n3. set steps to 20 in `BasicScheduler`;\n4. drag the `sampler` input of `SamplerCustomAdvanced` from the turbo sampler\n   over to `KSamplerSelect`, which is right next to it.\n\nSame order in reverse to come back. I ran this switch end to end and the graph\nsamples 20 steps exactly as before. An untouched 20-step copy also ships as\n`06-text-to-video-base-20steps.json`.\n\nWorth doing when turbo gives you a plastic face or a over-sharpened texture and\nthe strength dial did not help.\n"
      ]
    },
